Changelog: 17-10-2026 07:44:51

Added. Apply makes the portal bump itself when the push of step 1 needs one (apps.toml changed since the last
push: an app added, removed or moved, or a never-released pin moved along): portal/VERSION and its copies go to
the lowest version CI accepts, with the portal's CHANGELOG entry listing the changed pins, and the log says so.
Before, Apply only said that step 2's Bump portal had to come first, and the owner's release of 17-10-2026 was
committed without it and failed ci-local on check_manifest. The portal release itself stays step 2.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 06:41:05

Added. Bump portal done before step 1's push (an app added to apps.toml needs the bump in the push that adds it)
shows step 1's commands with the bump in them: git add, the release commit (amended when it is already made and
not pushed, as on 17-10-2026), ci-local and push, then the app tags; the portal release's commands come once every
app's version is tagged (plan.py: untagged_releases, unpushed_release_commit, app_commands with amend). Before,
Bump portal always showed the portal release's commands, also while the portal could not be released yet.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 06:29:57

Fixed. Pin no longer closes the window when tools/pin_app.py refuses an entry: it says so in the log ("not
pinned (...)") and pins the others. And pin_app itself now adds the sha256 line a new app's entry lacks until its
first release, which is what closed the window on the owner's Pin of fan-control-v0.1.0.

Changelog: 16-10-2026 11:57:04

Fixed. When apps.toml changed since the last push, the commands after step 1's Apply start with "First: step 2's
Bump portal (portal x.y.z)": before, they began with git commit, so a release committed without the portal bump
failed CI on the push (plan.py: app_commands takes the portal version the push needs).

Changelog: 16-10-2026 05:54:31

Added. Step 1 ticks every app that needs a release by itself, when the window opens and on each reload; Tick what
needs a release still ticks them all again. Refresh keeps your own ticks and unticks, and ticks an app that needs a
release only since the last refresh. A core that already holds its changes stays unticked, as before.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 23:22:38

Added. Semantic versioning (owner's decision, 15-10-2026): the version step 1 offers first follows the CHANGELOG
entries since the app's last tag, counted from that tag: Breaking. asks for a major release, Added. for a minor one,
Fixed. (or an entry without a kind, from before the rule) for a patch; core's entries count for the apps that carry
a core change. A VERSION bumped further already, or a first release, still comes first. Step 2 offers the higher of
CI's minimum and the portal's own: its CHANGELOG entries and apps.toml since the last portal tag, so an app added and
pushed before the portal release still asks for a minor portal release. The version box says why in its tooltip
(plan.py: version_choices, portal_suggestion).

Changelog: 15-10-2026 23:13:51

Added. Step 1's Apply also runs check_manifest.py against the last push, and says when apps.toml changed since then (an app
added, removed or moved to another section, not only a pin that moves along) so that the push needs a portal bump
too: before, only check_versions.py ran there, and a release push failed CI on the portal bump. The push commands of
both steps run tools/ci-local.sh first on Linux (tools/ci-local.sh && git push): no push when a CI job fails.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 21:11:49

A command line under the steps (command_line.py), in a splitter with them, so the commands a step shows run without a
separate terminal (owner's request). A command runs with bash -lc in the checkout, in the folder the last cd left, and
its output streams in as it runs (colour codes removed). Commands lists the commands the last Apply, Bump portal or
Withdraw showed, without their comments, to put one on the line. Up and Down go through the history, kept in the
settings (200 commands). Stop or Ctrl+C sends Ctrl+C to the command's own process group (setsid), so a job it started
in the background ends too, and kills it if it has not ended 3 seconds later; clear and Clear empty the output. Input
is closed and git is told not to prompt, to use no editor and no pager, so no command waits for an answer that cannot
come. After each command the window reads the checkout again, as Refresh does. Tests: output, cd, exit codes, closed
input, Stop with a background job, history, the commands offered, the pane in the window.

